## A Growing Concern: Food Recalls on the Rise

It's time we faced it: our food supply chain seems to be on shaky ground. With 327 recalls already this year, we're on par to surpass last year's record-high numbers. Of these, 90 were Class I recalls--indicating a reasonable probability of causing serious health issues or death.

## Not So Fresh: Brands Leading the Recall Charts

Let's talk names. "Moonlight Packing Corporation" and "Albertsons Companies LLC" top our list of repeat offenders, with 11 recalls each this year. Out of these, the majority are classified as Class I recalls. When your produce or products consistently land in the 'danger zone', it's a clear sign that oversight measures are failing.

## Monthly Trends and What They Tell Us

Breaking it down further, October 2025 saw a staggering 146 recalls--an alarming peak that raises questions about quality assurance processes leading into the holiday season. Alarmingly, 'Allergen' issues accounted for the highest share--184 instances this year--highlighting a dire need for better labeling practices.

## Recent Recalls: A Snapshot

Here are some notable players in this month's recall roundup:

- "Kirkland Signature Traditional Madeleines" by Costco Wholesale Corporation: Recall due to undeclared hazelnut and soy lecithin, a Class I recall.
- "Bombay Kitchen brand Tikka Sev" by Ethnic Foods Inc: Containing undeclared artificial colors, falling under Class II.
- "SALUTE SEASONINGS; Ground All Spice" by Unistel Industries Inc.: Found to be contaminated with Salmonella, a hazardous Class I recall.

These examples underscore the need for diligence in our food production and distribution systems.

## The Takeaway

The recent data reveal systemic safety lapses that are risking consumer health on a large scale. As consumers, our best defense is vigilance: pay attention to recall announcements, scrutinize food labels, and hold companies accountable for their missteps.

## Brands to Watch Closely

We owe it to ourselves and our families to be informed. The following brands have consistently appeared as repeat offenders, and their products should be approached with due diligence:

- Moonlight Packing Corporation
- Albertsons Companies LLC
- Ambriola Co., Inc.
- Everpress Juice
- Diva Fam Inc
- Black Sheep Egg Company, LLC
- CHETAK NEW YORK LLC
- Made Fresh Salads Inc

Stay sharp and shelf check wisely.
